    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village sprang up around 1845 when William Day built a house and his son opened the first store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nineveh_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers included Scotch‑Irish families like the Millikens and Grays, who farmed the fertile valleys of Greene County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nineveh_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nineveh developed modestly with a brick schoolhouse, a creamery founded in 1875, sawmills, and flour mills serving the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nineveh_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Located in Morris Township, Nineveh sits along Brown's Fork of Ten Mile Creek in southwestern Pennsylvania.",
